# Git: commit / "praise be" / "say my name" mean commit + push

When the user asks to **commit**, says **praise be**, says **say my name**, asks to **save to GitHub**, **push**, or uses a similarly short request to persist work:

1. Stage the relevant changes (`git add` as appropriate, or all if they did not narrow scope).
2. Create the commit with a clear message.
3. **Immediately run `git push`** to `origin` for the **current branch** (unless they explicitly asked for commit-only or named a different remote/branch).

If there is **nothing to commit**, check for **unpushed commits** (`git log origin/<branch>..HEAD` or equivalent) and **push** those.

Report push failures (auth, permissions, branch protection) and what to fix; do not assume push succeeded without verifying exit code.

## Confirmation phrases

After the workflow completes **successfully** (commit + push OK, or branch was already fully synced with nothing pending after you verified), end the user-facing reply with **exactly one** of these lines on its own, based on what the user said:

| User said | Success line |
|-----------|--------------|
| **say my name** (or a clear variant) | `heisenberg` |
| **praise be**, **commit**, **save to GitHub**, **push**, or other persist requests | `blessed be the fruit` |

If both triggers appear in the same message, prefer **heisenberg**.

If the workflow **failed** (e.g. push rejected), do **not** use either phrase; explain the failure instead.
